\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Bidding When Running Feeder Strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 If you're running the Feeder Strategy (Standard Shopping + Non-Brand Search as cold traffic acquisition with Performance Max as middle/bottom funnel remarketing), your bidding approach needs to create a clear hierarchy between your campaigns.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Core Principle: PMax tROAS Must Be Higher Than Shopping\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When I say "higher," I mean your Performance Max campaign should have a 
\f0\b more aggressive (higher) tROAS target
\f1\b0  than your Standard Shopping campaigns. This creates the desired behavior where Standard Shopping captures cold traffic at a lower efficiency threshold, while Performance Max focuses on warm audiences who convert at higher rates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 The 20% Rule\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Set your Performance Max tROAS at least 
\f0\b 20% higher
\f1\b0  than your Standard Shopping tROAS. Calculate this as 20% of your Shopping tROAS added on top.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Example:
\f1\b0  If your Standard Shopping tROAS is set to 200%, then your Performance Max should be at least 240% (200% \'d7 1.20 = 240%).\

\f0\b Feeder Strategy tROAS Examples:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 {{\NeXTGraphic ba0bac0cb57b4da283569467c0a6a60f48a20610c8704f29b325ed5e059afdee.png \width9160 \height3200 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u55357 \u56481  
\f0\b Pro Tip:
\f1\b0  
\f2\i Play around with these percentage differences. We find 20% higher to be a good starting point, but some accounts perform better with 25-30% separation. Test and cross-reference with your backend new customer acquisition data.
\f1\i0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Why This Works\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The idea behind the Feeder Strategy is that you're acquiring cold, new customers through Standard Shopping campaigns, and Performance Max becomes your middle-of-funnel and bottom-of-funnel remarketing back channel. It "feeds" on the warm audience traffic pool of those new customers or cold traffic generated from the Shopping campaign who didn't buy right away\'97those who clicked but didn't convert that same day.\
By setting a higher tROAS on PMax, you're telling Google: "Only spend money on highly efficient conversions." This naturally pushes PMax toward warm, high-intent traffic while your Shopping campaigns do the heavy lifting of new customer acquisition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Budget Allocation for Feeder Strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The goal is to push more budget into Standard Shopping for new customer acquisition (cold traffic) and use Performance Max as a remarketing middle-of-funnel component.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Recommended Budget Split:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 70-80% of budget
\f1\b0  allocated to Standard Shopping\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 20-30% of budget
\f1\b0  allocated to Performance Max\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 This is not a hard rule. If your PMax is performing exceedingly well at 30% budget allocation, don't be afraid to increase it. The key is cross-referencing with what you see on new customer acquisition on the backend.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Transitioning to Feeder Strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When we implement the Feeder Strategy, we don't normally hard-switch to 80% Shopping / 20% PMax right away. Usually it's a slow transition:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Gradually ramp up Standard Shopping budget\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Simultaneously decrease PMax spend as Shopping starts to pick up\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Monitor backend new customer acquisition metrics throughout\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Adjust the ratio based on actual performance data\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Bidding When NOT Running Feeder Strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 If you're not running the Feeder Strategy and Performance Max is your primary campaign (or you're just getting started), here's how to approach bidding and optimization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Phase 1: Learning Phase (First 30 Days)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 In the beginning, you're typically going to bid to get volume. The goal is reaching 
\f0\b 30-50 conversions per month
\f1\b0 . During this phase, expect unprofitable or underwhelming performance\'97this is normal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Starting Bid Strategy Options:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Max Conversion Value
\f1\b0  (no tROAS cap) - Standard approach\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Let the campaign run for 
\f0\b 1-4 weeks
\f1\b0  to collect enough data before making changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Phase 2: Setting Your Initial tROAS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 After the learning phase, switch to tROAS bidding. 
\f0\b Critical rule:
\f1\b0  Set the tROAS to what the campaign is 
\f2\i already achieving
\f1\i0 , not what you wish it would hit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \uc0\u9888 \u65039  Warning: If your campaign is hitting 250% ROAS, you cannot just flip to a 400% tROAS target. This will kill the campaign. Smart Bidding doesn't respond well to dramatic changes.
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b Correct Approach:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Check your current ROAS performance (e.g., 200%)\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Set tROAS to current performance OR slightly higher (180-200%)\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Wait 1-2 weeks for the campaign to stabilize\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Incrementally increase tROAS toward your goal\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Phase 3: Optimization - Two Paths Forward\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Once your campaign has stabilized and is hitting around your tROAS goal while generating conversions, you have two optimization options:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Option A: Scale Volume at Current Performance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you're satisfied with your current ROAS and want more conversions at that efficiency level, start increasing your budget in 
\f0\b 10-20% increments
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Option B: Improve Efficiency\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you need better ROAS and can't live with current performance, start incrementally increasing your tROAS target in 
\f0\b 10-20% increments.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \uc0\u9888 \u65039  Caution: If you're only getting 30 conversions per month, increasing tROAS too aggressively can hurt you. If Google decreases that 30 conversion volume, you could suffocate the campaign. Be confident before making this change.
\f1\b0 \
\uc0\u55357 \u56481  
\f0\b Pro Tip:
\f1\b0  
\f2\i Normally you're either adjusting bids OR adjusting budgets\'97not both at the same time. Make one change, then wait up to a week or longer to see what happened. If you adjust bid and budget simultaneously, you'll second-guess yourself on what caused any problems.
\f1\i0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Controlling ROAS With Budget\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Budget is one of your primary levers for controlling Performance Max behavior. Here's how to use it effectively:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 When Increasing Budget\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If your campaign is hitting your desired ROAS goal and you are limited by budget, you might notice when you increase budget, 
\f0\b the ROAS will dip
\f1\b0 . This is due to Google expanding your audience reach.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 What to do:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Give it 1-2 weeks to work the new data out at this higher spend range\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Monitor performance\'97sometimes it stabilizes back to target\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If it doesn't recover, decrease budget back (see below)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 When Decreasing Budget\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If your campaign struggles to hit ROAS goal with increased budget, start decreasing your budget back. This will 
\f0\b force Smart Bidding to decrease your audience reach
\f1\b0  to what it knows works best\'97the low-hanging fruit.\
Sometimes a smaller, more focused budget delivers better efficiency than a larger budget that forces Google to expand into less qualified audiences.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Golden Rules for Smart Bidding\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

